    96	Supersymmetry makes the nonzero spectra of `(i,N)` and `(i+1,N-1)` coincide, so the invariant
    97	object is the Laplacian of the whole internal-degree-`j` complex (part C). 5.4 `g` is a
    98	**new parameter**, not `Delta_N` (D-macaulay-gap), and no analogue of Fact 7.1 (C-107) is
    99	known for it.
   100	
   101	**Step 6 (support: Betti information lives at `N <= reg`).** `reg(R/I) =
   102	max{j-i : beta_{i,j} != 0}` (D-saturation-regularity-stable-range) and `beta_{i,j} = 0` for
   103	`i > n+1` (Hilbert syzygy); since the block label is `N = j-i`:
   104	
   105	> **KB2.** `dim ker(L_W|(i,N)) = 0` for every `i` whenever `N > reg(R/I)`.
   106	
   107	Part A checks this as a prediction out to `N = 8,7,7,6,5` against `reg = 1,2,2,1,1`. This
   108	inverts the seed's geometry: arm A wants the *stable* range `N >= reg` (C-011); arm B has all
   109	its content at `N <= reg`.
   110	
   111	**Step 7 (relation to `Syz(I)_N` and the §5 rows).** 7.1 `Syz(I)_N = ker Phi_N`
   112	(D-syzygy-module, C-052) is the non-minimal degree-`N` piece of the *tuple's* syzygies, of
   113	dimension `sum_j dim R_{N-m_j} - dim I_N`, fixed by the Hilbert function; it is not a Betti
   114	number. 7.2 `beta_{1,j}(R/I)` counts minimal generators of `I` in degree `j` and
   115	`beta_{2,j} = dim(Syz / m Syz)_j` counts minimal first syzygies: arm B performs exactly the
   116	minimalisation `Phi_N` does not. 7.3 *Caution*: the first nonzero row is free. For `I` with no
   117	linear forms, `L_W|(1,1) = 2 - X X^†` with `X f = sum_k (∂_k f) ⊗ e_k` the gradient on `I_2`,
   118	and `sum_k ||∂_k f||^2 = <f, N̂ f> = 2||f||^2`, so `X^†X = 2` on `I_2` and
   119	`beta_{1,2} = dim I_2` — a rank, readable from the input (confirmed in every family:
   120	3, 2, 5, 14). The first genuinely non-input block is `i = 2`.
   121	
   122	**Step 8 (the other supercharge: the seed's `H` is a Koszul block).** With `d` fermionic modes
   123	and `Q_f := sum_j a^†(f_j) ⊗ gamma_j`, `Q_f^2 = 0` and the `i=0` block of
   124	`Q_f Q_f^† + Q_f^† Q_f` is `sum_j a^†(f_j) a(f_j) = H`: the seed Hamiltonian is the bottom
   125	block of a supersymmetric Koszul Laplacian on the *generators*, and this one needs no
   126	projector — a genuine `max_j m_j`-body boson–fermion Hamiltonian (D-k-body, D-few-mode). Its
   127	higher blocks compute `H_i(f;R) = Tor_i^S(C,R)`, `S = C[y_1..y_d]`, `y_j |-> f_j`, which
   128	vanish for `i>0` exactly when `f` is a regular sequence; these are **not** `beta_{i,j}(R/I)`.
   129	Untested here (mutation M4).
   130	
   131	**Step 9 (what a quantum algorithm block-encodes; and one honest positive).** 9.1 Sparse
   132	access to `H_N` gives `P_0` by QSVT at `O((alpha_BE/Delta_N) log(1/eps))` block-encoding uses
   133	(C-056, D-qsvt); `Q_W` costs `n+1` row-computable `a_k` (C-054) times one `P_0`, and `Q_W^†`
   134	costs `n+1` `a_k^†` sandwiched by two `P_0`s, so one use of `L_W` is
   135	`O(n · alpha_BE/Delta_N)` uses of the `H_N` block encoding with `||L_W|| <= j`. A DQC1-style
   136	estimate (D-dqc1-style-estimate) of `Tr(Pi_{ker L_W})/dim` then costs
   137	`Õ(n (alpha_BE/Delta_N)(j/g_{i,N}) eps^{-2})` and returns the **normalised Betti fraction**
   138	`beta_{i,j}/(HF(j-i) C(n+1,i))`, never `beta`. 9.2 `beta_{i,j}(R/I)` is an invariant of `I`,
   139	so `dim ker L_W` does not move when the generating tuple changes — the
   140	presentation-independence `Delta_N` lacks (D-macaulay-gap pitfall (i), C-106). Arm B's
   141	*output* is better posed than arm A's; only the cost parameters are presentation-dependent.
   142	
