   260	
   261	## 3. Against the north star (PRD §2, criteria 1–5)
   262	
   263	**1. Problem.** `NORM-BETTI(i)`: input, homogeneous `f_1..f_d` in `n+1` variables with
   264	`poly(n)` monomials and `poly(n)`-bit coefficients, `i` and `j` in unary with `j <= reg`
   265	(D-input-model); promises `Delta_N/alpha_BE >= 1/poly(n)` at `N = j-i` and
   266	`g_{i,N}/(N+i) >= 1/poly(n)`; output, an additive-`eps` estimate of
   267	`beta_{i,j}(R/I)/(HF(j-i) C(n+1,i))`. Decision version `GAPPED-BETTI(i)`: decide
   268	`beta_{i,j} = 0` versus `>= 1` under the same promises. The input model is the seed's,
   269	unchanged; `reg` must be supplied (K-KB10). The output is a presentation-independent
   270	invariant (Step 9.2) — better posed than any `Delta_N`-style target in arm A.
   271	
   272	**2. Classical baseline.** Not Macaulay2 — that is the classical memo's trap "comparing
   273	against the wrong classical algorithm". Structural baselines: minimal free resolutions by
   274	Schreyer frames and Hilbert-function pruning (La Scala–Stillman, *J. Symbolic Comput.*
   275	26(4) 409–431 (1998), DOI 10.1006/jsco.1998.0221; practical `n = 10..40`, `10^3..10^6`
   276	syzygies) and finite-dimensional syzygies by fast linear algebra (Neiger–Schost,
   277	*J. Complexity* 60, 101502 (2020), DOI 10.1016/j.jco.2020.101502, arXiv:1912.01848 — two
   278	authors, not "et al.", correcting classical memo [R31]; `O(m D^{omega-1} + n D^omega log D)`).
   279	The baseline that actually competes is **stochastic Chebyshev/Lanczos eigenvalue counting**
   280	on the same sparse Koszul matrices: Di Napoli–Polizzi–Saad, *Numer. Linear Algebra Appl.*
   281	23(4) 674–692 (2016), DOI 10.1002/nla.2048, arXiv:1308.4275 — polynomial filtering plus
   282	Hutchinson traces counts eigenvalues in an interval, i.e. exactly the normalised nullity of
   283	`L_W`, and it needs the same `P_0`, obtainable classically by a Chebyshev filter on the same
   284	sparse `H_N`. So both sides pay the `Delta_N` promise, one inside a QSVT and one inside a
   285	Krylov iteration on length-`C(N+n,n)` vectors; the residual comparison is `n log N` qubits
   286	versus `M_N` words, which PRD D15 / fact SP-0 (C-264) refuses as a space separation.
   287	
   288	**3. Quantum algorithm.** Step 9.1: `Õ(n (alpha_BE/Delta_N)(j/g_{i,N}) eps^{-2})` uses of the
   289	sparse `H_N` block encoding (`eps^{-1}` with amplitude estimation on a pure-state version);
   290	`O(n log N)` bosonic qubits plus `n+1` fermionic. Proven margin over the baseline: none. The
   291	only structural advantage is `||L_W|| <= j` (Step 4.4) — the Koszul layer is cheap, all cost
   292	is in `P_0`, i.e. in arm A's promises. **Arm B cannot beat arm A; it strictly contains it.**
   293	
   294	**4. Dequantization audit.** This decides the arm.
   295	(a) Via Hochster (part d) the monomial case is exactly normalised Betti number estimation for
   296	a simplicial complex, and Apers–Gribling–Sen–Szabó, *Quantum* 7, 1202 (2023),
   297	DOI 10.22331/q-2023-12-06-1202, arXiv:2211.09618, estimate the `k`-th **normalised** Betti
   298	number to additive `eps` classically by path-integral Monte Carlo in
   299	`n^{O(gamma^{-1/2} log(1/eps))}`, `gamma` the combinatorial-Laplacian gap (improving to
   300	`(n/lambda_max)^{O(...)}` on clique complexes, matching the quantum runtime when
   301	`gamma = Omega(1)` and `k = Omega(n)`). Our `gamma` is `g/(N+i)`. Plainly: **at a constant
   302	normalised Betti gap the estimator is dequantized to quasi-polynomial time; the only surviving
   303	window is `g/(N+i) = 1/poly(n)` with `eps = 1/poly(n)`, where the classical bound degrades to
   304	`n^{O(poly(n))}` and the quantum stays polynomial** — exactly the window in which no lower
   305	bound on `g` is known (K-KB6).
   306	(b) Berry et al., *PRX Quantum* 5, 010319 (2024), DOI 10.1103/PRXQuantum.5.010319,
   307	arXiv:2209.13581: exponentially large chain dimension **and** exponentially large Betti number
   308	are necessary, neither sufficient; super-quadratic advantage only for multiplicative error with
   309	growing Betti number. Our chain dimension `HF(N) C(n+1,i)` and `beta` can both be exponential
   310	(families with `reg = Theta(n)`, e.g. D-boolean-ideal), and part (e) gives a `1/poly` fraction
   311	at fixed `i` — the necessary conditions are reachable, but multiplicative error is blocked by (c).
   312	(c) Schmidhuber–Lloyd, *PRX Quantum* 4, 040349 (2023), DOI 10.1103/PRXQuantum.4.040349,
   313	arXiv:2209.14286: exact Betti numbers #P-hard, multiplicative approximation NP-hard, in the
   314	regime where quantum TDA performs best. Through Hochster this transfers verbatim to
   315	`beta_{i,j}` of Stanley–Reisner ideals, agreeing with classical memo §10 and
   316	D-hardness-anchors. Only the normalised additive problem survives, as C-093 says for `HF`.
   317	(d) The best product of this lane: King–Kohler, *Gapped Clique Homology on weighted graphs is
   318	QMA_1-hard and contained in QMA*, arXiv:2311.17234, FOCS 2024, 493–504,
   319	DOI 10.1109/FOCS61266.2024.00039 (also SIAM J. Comput. FOCS24 section,
   320	DOI 10.1137/24M1710243); Crichigno–Kohler, *Clique Homology is QMA_1-hard*, arXiv:2209.11793,
   321	*Nature Communications* 15, 9846 (2024), DOI 10.1038/s41467-024-54118-z; Hayakawa,
   322	arXiv:2608.02726 (2026, arXiv only), unweighted gapped clique homology is QMA_1-complete.
   323	Their instances are clique complexes, i.e. Stanley–Reisner complexes of edge ideals, so
   324	`GAPPED-BETTI` **inherits QMA_1-hardness** — a stronger hardness anchor for an
   325	algebraic-geometry problem than the seed's only candidate (C-124, conjectural), and
   326	simultaneously a negative for the speedup.
   327	(e) Novelty caution. Cade–Crichigno, arXiv:2107.00011, *Quantum* 8, 1325 (2024),
   328	DOI 10.22331/q-2024-04-30-1325, already formalise "N=2 SUSY local Hamiltonian, ground energy
   329	zero iff a cohomology group is nontrivial" and prove QMA-completeness; Crichigno,
   330	arXiv:2011.01239 (arXiv only), shows the Witten index is #P-complete. The
   331	SUSY-computes-homology mechanism is **not new**; the algebro-geometric instance is.
   332	*Correction to carry*: Gyurik–Cade–Dunjko, *Quantum* 6, 855 (2022),
   333	DOI 10.22331/q-2022-11-10-855, arXiv:2005.02607, prove DQC1-hardness of **low-lying spectral
   334	density estimation**, *not* of normalised Betti number estimation, and leave the restriction to
   335	clique-complex Laplacians explicitly open; any row citing them for "normalised BNE is
   336	DQC1-hard" is mis-citing.
   337	(f) Trap audit (classical memo): dense rank — avoided; changing the output — a normalised
   338	fraction, said so; hidden state preparation — the maximally mixed block state needs `P_0`,
   339	priced; exponentially small overlap — **not triggered**, part (e) gives `1/poly` at fixed `i`,
   340	the first time in the campaign; hiding exponential `N` — inapplicable (KB2); ignoring gap
   341	closing — triggered (K-KB6); ignoring classical preconditioning — triggered (K-KB9).
   342	
   343	**5. Heuristic attack.** Weak, and that is itself a finding: by Step 4.1 the *whole* Koszul
   344	Laplacian is the free Hamiltonian `N̂ + F̂`, a Bose–Fermi Hubbard model with no interaction, and
   345	all content sits in `P_0`, a QSVT projector rather than an interaction term. There is no natural
   346	condensed-matter Hamiltonian whose ground degeneracy is a Betti table, in contrast to C-024 (the
   347	conic as the Law–Pu–Bigelow spin-mixing Hamiltonian). Two survivors. (i) *Monomial ideals*:
   348	`H_N` is diagonal (C-111), `P_0` is a computational-basis projector, and `L_W` is an honest
   349	sparse number-conserving hopping Hamiltonian on the standard monomials tensored with a
   350	Jordan–Wigner fermion register on `n+1` sites. Smallest demonstration: the Stanley–Reisner ideal
   351	of the 4-cycle, `I = (z_0z_2, z_1z_3)`, block `(i=1,N=1)` — **one boson in 4 modes (dual rail)
   352	plus one fermion in 4 modes**, a 16-level two-particle problem whose zero-energy degeneracy is
   353	`beta_{1,2} = 2`; the 7-cycle at `(i=3,N=1)` is 245-dimensional with degeneracy 35 and
   354	normalised gap 0.146, still tabletop. A demonstration, not a computation (K-KB7).
   355	(ii) *The other supercharge* (Step 8): `Q_f = sum_j a^†(f_j) ⊗ gamma_j`, a genuine
   356	`max_j m_j`-body boson–fermion interaction with no projector whose `i=0` block is the seed
   357	Hamiltonian (for quadrics, spin-mixing plus a fermion). It is the only natively physical object
   358	this lane found, and it computes Koszul homology of the sequence, not the Betti table.
   359	
   360	---
   361	
   362	## 4. Killers
   363	
   364	**K-KB1 (support collapse; F3).** `beta_{i,j} = 0` for `j-i > reg(R/I)` (Step 6, part a), so
   365	`N <= reg` and the seed's exponential-compression argument applies only where regularity grows
   366	with `n`; the twisted cubic, the rational normal curves and every determinantal example in the
   367	seed's survey have `reg(R/I) <= 2`. Surviving: arm B needs `reg = Theta(n)`, and
   368	D-boolean-ideal / D-clause-ideal are the campaign's only such families — exactly the #P-hard
   369	ones. Severity high.
   370	
   371	**K-KB2 (the naive compression is information-free).** `P_0 L P_0 = (N+i) P_0` exactly
   372	(Step 4.3, part b): "block-encode the projected Koszul Laplacian" returns a scalar. The
   373	algorithm must use `Q_W^† = sum_k Z_k ⊗ c_k`, so each application of `L_W` costs a full QSVT
   374	projector at `alpha_BE/Delta_N`. Surviving: arm B ⊇ arm A in cost, never the cheaper arm.
   375	Severity decisive for cost accounting.
   376	
   377	**K-KB3 (the problem is quantum TDA).** Hochster plus part d: the multigraded blocks of `L_W`
